WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.513
Picture the restaurant from last time, now with a second room.

2
00:00:06.513 --> 00:00:09.744
Next door, the owner has opened a small test kitchen.

3
00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:13.245
New dishes are tried there on a few regulars first.

4
00:00:13.245 --> 00:00:15.323
On Tuesday, a new soup is a hit.

5
00:00:15.467 --> 00:00:18.809
On Friday, the main kitchen cooks it from memory.

6
00:00:18.809 --> 00:00:20.104
A little more salt.

7
00:00:20.104 --> 00:00:21.332
A different stock.

8
00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:23.927
The regulars ask why it tastes wrong.

9
00:00:23.927 --> 00:00:26.719
An easy trap: it feels like the same soup.

10
00:00:26.719 --> 00:00:28.847
Same name, same pot, same cooks.

11
00:00:29.533 --> 00:00:30.966
So here is our question.

12
00:00:30.966 --> 00:00:35.563
How does a dish move from one room to the other, without changing on the way?

13
00:00:36.933 --> 00:00:39.564
Software teams keep the same pair of rooms.

14
00:00:39.564 --> 00:00:44.029
Each running copy of the app, with one purpose, is called an environment.

15
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:47.875
Dev is the test kitchen, where new versions land first.

16
00:00:47.875 --> 00:00:51.616
In the lab it runs one copy, and answers Hello from dev.

17
00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:54.922
Prod is the main room, the copy your users see.

18
00:00:54.922 --> 00:00:57.445
It runs four copies, with more memory.

19
00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.749
Both rooms run from one board.

20
00:00:59.749 --> 00:01:07.128
Shared files sit in one folder, and each environment gets an overlay: a folder of small changes on top.

21
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.646
Each overlay also names the version to run.

22
00:01:10.646 --> 00:01:14.220
An image is your app, packed with everything it needs.

23
00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.140
Its tag is the version label.

24
00:01:16.333 --> 00:01:22.228
Argo CD, the manager from last time, deploys each overlay to its own namespace.

25
00:01:22.933 --> 00:01:26.109
Argo CD needs one Application per environment.

26
00:01:26.109 --> 00:01:29.354
You could copy the file and change three lines.

27
00:01:29.354 --> 00:01:31.977
That works, until someone forgets one.

28
00:01:32.133 --> 00:01:35.177
An ApplicationSet writes those copies for you.

29
00:01:35.177 --> 00:01:39.080
It has a template: an ordinary Application with gaps in it.

30
00:01:39.267 --> 00:01:41.185
And a list of words for the gaps.

31
00:01:41.185 --> 00:01:44.848
In the restaurant, a blank board, filled in once for each room.

32
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.233
Apply it, and two Applications appear: hello-devops-dev, and hello-devops-prod.

33
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:55.292
Delete the ApplicationSet, and its apps go too, with everything they deployed.

34
00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.071
Now the real question.

35
00:01:58.071 --> 00:02:00.708
Version 1.2.0 runs well in dev.

36
00:02:00.708 --> 00:02:03.004
How do you move it to prod?

37
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:04.570
Four answers.

38
00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:06.584
Rebuild the image for prod.

39
00:02:06.584 --> 00:02:08.076
Change prod by hand.

40
00:02:08.076 --> 00:02:12.104
Copy the tag into the prod folder with a pull request.

41
00:02:12.104 --> 00:02:13.148
Or click Sync.

42
00:02:16.267 --> 00:02:17.860
Let's be fair to each one.

43
00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:22.211
A rebuild sounds harmless, but it can produce a slightly different app.

44
00:02:22.211 --> 00:02:23.988
A new soup, with an old name.

45
00:02:24.133 --> 00:02:28.897
A hand edit with kubectl skips the board, so Argo CD undoes it.

46
00:02:29.067 --> 00:02:31.890
And Sync only applies what git already says.

47
00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:34.200
Git still says the old tag for prod.

48
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.271
That leaves the pull request.

49
00:02:36.271 --> 00:02:38.851
Promotion is a small text change in git.

50
00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.316
Let's replay the whole journey, from the start.

51
00:02:42.467 --> 00:02:43.345
Step one.

52
00:02:43.345 --> 00:02:48.709
CI builds the image once, when you push the tag v1.2.0.

53
00:02:48.867 --> 00:02:49.680
Step two.

54
00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:53.749
A pull request sets the dev overlay to 1.2.0.

55
00:02:53.749 --> 00:02:57.185
You merge it, and Argo CD deploys dev.

56
00:02:57.333 --> 00:02:58.101
Step three.

57
00:02:58.101 --> 00:03:01.592
You test dev, the way the regulars taste the soup.

58
00:03:01.733 --> 00:03:02.494
Step four.

59
00:03:02.494 --> 00:03:06.297
A second pull request sets prod to the same 1.2.0.

60
00:03:06.297 --> 00:03:11.545
Someone reviews and merges it: the head chef's signature on the card.

61
00:03:11.733 --> 00:03:15.047
Argo CD sees the change, and deploys prod.

62
00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.154
Notice what never happened.

63
00:03:17.154 --> 00:03:19.180
The image was never rebuilt.

64
00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:22.002
Prod runs the exact bytes that dev ran.

65
00:03:22.002 --> 00:03:23.739
Build once, deploy many.

66
00:03:23.933 --> 00:03:26.022
And you get a clean undo for free.

67
00:03:26.022 --> 00:03:30.752
Press Revert on the merged pull request, and a new one puts the old tag back.

68
00:03:31.267 --> 00:03:32.106
So the rule.

69
00:03:32.106 --> 00:03:35.396
A rebuilt image is a new soup with an old name.

70
00:03:35.396 --> 00:03:37.985
Build once, and move that same image.

71
00:03:39.067 --> 00:03:42.735
This only works if a tag always means the same image.

72
00:03:42.735 --> 00:03:43.981
So let's break it.

73
00:03:43.981 --> 00:03:45.849
Set prod to the tag latest.

74
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.588
On Monday, latest points at one image.

75
00:03:48.733 --> 00:03:51.044
On Wednesday, it points at another.

76
00:03:51.044 --> 00:03:54.016
Git did not change at all, but what runs did.

77
00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:57.532
Ordering latest is like ordering the chef's surprise.

78
00:03:57.532 --> 00:03:59.293
Lovely at a birthday dinner.

79
00:03:59.293 --> 00:04:01.431
Not for the main room on a Friday.

80
00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:08.604
So the point is: with latest, git no longer tells you what runs, and a revert cannot bring the old version back.

81
00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:10.849
A version like 1.2.0 is good.

82
00:04:10.849 --> 00:04:19.117
A digest is stronger: a fingerprint worked out from the image's contents, so nobody can move it to a different image.

83
00:04:20.733 --> 00:04:21.976
A few guardrails.

84
00:04:21.976 --> 00:04:25.120
Prod moves only by a reviewed pull request.

85
00:04:25.267 --> 00:04:26.310
Never latest.

86
00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:29.120
Use a version, or better, a digest.

87
00:04:29.267 --> 00:04:36.112
An add-on, Argo CD Image Updater, can write new tags for you, but it commits with no signature.

88
00:04:36.112 --> 00:04:40.075
My advice: dev at most, and keep prod on pull requests.

89
00:04:40.267 --> 00:04:43.315
And nobody writes the safe's code on the board.

90
00:04:43.315 --> 00:04:49.283
A password in git is no longer a secret, so commit only locked secrets, or pointers to them.

91
00:04:50.067 --> 00:04:51.742
So the moral of the story.

92
00:04:51.742 --> 00:04:55.349
Build once, and move the same image, with a signed card.

93
00:04:55.533 --> 00:04:58.441
Dev is the test kitchen, prod the main room.

94
00:04:58.441 --> 00:05:00.819
One blank board, filled in per room.

95
00:05:00.819 --> 00:05:02.802
A dish moves as a signed card.

96
00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:06.568
And a rebuild, or latest, is a new soup with an old name.

97
00:05:07.267 --> 00:05:08.301
A quick check.

98
00:05:08.301 --> 00:05:13.102
You add a third entry, env staging, to the ApplicationSet's list.

99
00:05:13.102 --> 00:05:14.284
What do you get?

100
00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.814
A third Application, written from the same template.

101
00:05:20.814 --> 00:05:23.835
Nobody copied a file, so nobody forgot a line.
